<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Text on STEP Batch 12</title><link>https://step-12.github.io/tags/text/</link><description>Recent content in Text on STEP Batch 12</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en_US</language><lastBuildDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 17:49:03 +0530</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://step-12.github.io/tags/text/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Drawing Text</title><link>https://step-12.github.io/posts/raylib/drawing_text/</link><pubDate>Wed, 21 Oct 2026 09:00:00 +0530</pubDate><guid>https://step-12.github.io/posts/raylib/drawing_text/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="drawing-text"&gt;Drawing text&lt;a class="anchor" href="#drawing-text"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;We&amp;rsquo;ve spent a lot of time drawing things such as rectangles, circles and lines.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;But what if we want to put some words on the screen?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Raylib provides &lt;code&gt;DrawText&lt;/code&gt; for this.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;The basic API looks like this:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-js" data-lang="js"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DrawText&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;&amp;#34;Hello, Raylib!&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;24&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;WHITE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;The arguments are:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;ol&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;The text to draw.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;The x-coordinate.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;The y-coordinate.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;The font size.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;The color.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ol&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-js" data-lang="js"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DrawText&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;&amp;#34;Hello&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;50&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;50&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;WHITE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DrawText&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;&amp;#34;Raylib&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;50&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;80&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;YELLOW&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;The two pieces of text can have different positions, sizes and colors.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>